Age (and other information) verification based on pki
Organizations such as the DMV would have a keypair used to sign merkle trees.
For example, the DMV might give you a tree with your name, ID# birthdate (or, even better,days after jan 1 1850 that you were born), and weight. They would then define a public schema to maintain the usability of the tree. They sign this tree with their private key so it can be verified with their public key. Then you could provide credentials along with the relevant parts of the merkel tree to prove whatever credentials you want. For things like age where you might just want to prove you are above or below some threshold (this is why we use the number of days since 1/1/1850) without actually revealing your birthdate, you can do a zero knowledge range proof. This altogether creates a credential where you can share only what is necessary.


Implementation details;
Using the zksk lib in python to generate commitments for zero knowledge proofs. This is how things like age will be saved. Other things where a zkp doesn't necessarily make sense (name, id#) would be stored as strings/numbers. These will then all be stored inside of a merkle tree (should be able to just make this myself using some lib with a cryptographic hash function). This tree is signed by the governing body using a pki system similar to ssl so it can be publically verified. The simple, initial implementation will have the verifier ask the prover for a set of credentials. The prover will provide either them or their commitments, depending on whether or not they are zkp values, along with the rest of the merkle tree parts that are necessary. The verifier will verify the merkel tree and the signature to make sure it all checks out, and return whether or not it does. 

In an actual setup for this system, I think new trees would need to be issued periodically to maintain security, but I think only the first one would need to be done "manually". Say along with your id tree at the dmv they give you some long key to decrypt your next one when they send it out. That message than contains your next one, so on and so on. If this ever gets compromised you'd need to get re-verified which would not be fun but not much different that losing your ID or getting your identity stolen in some other way.

The main motivation for this is the recent increase in organizations inflicting things like age verification on their users. I don't want to have to give everything a picture of my ID, but I also get why they want to verify age (although I very much suspect they don't just use if for )

